[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

How would you like to approach this? Feel free to take a moment to gather your thoughts first.

[candidate · 1:30] Great, let me play this back to make sure I've got it. Brew & Bean, 200 US locations, specialty coffee, and we've seen net margin compress from 24% to 6% over two years — that's an 18-point compression — while top line has grown 15%. So this is emphatically not a demand problem; this is a cost structure problem masquerading as a growth story. Revenue up, margin down, means cost is growing meaningfully faster than revenue — probably on the order of 20-25% over the same window if we back into it.

Here's how I'd structure it. First, I'd decompose profit into revenue and cost. On revenue I'd want to split price versus volume versus mix — specifically whether the 15% growth came from new store openings or same-store sales, because those have very different margin implications. If I had to guess from what we know, I'd say same-store traffic is up around 12% and the rest is new units, which would actually be a healthy picture.

Second, cost. I'd split fixed versus variable, then within variable I'd look at COGS — green coffee, dairy, cups, syrups — and within fixed I'd look at labor, rent, utilities, and corporate overhead.

Third, I'd look at external factors: commodity cycles, wage legislation, competitive discounting.

To start, do we have a cost breakdown as a percentage of revenue for both years? And ideally a menu-price history alongside it.
