[candidate · 6:10] This is clarifying, and it basically confirms the thesis. COGS is up 16 points of revenue, 42 to 58, overhead is up 2, labor flat. So 16 of my 18 points of margin compression sit in cost of goods. Labor and overhead are noise. And critically, menu prices have not moved in two years — so with 15% revenue growth on a flat 200-store base and flat pricing, this is 100% volume. Traffic is up roughly 15%, which is outstanding. They are selling meaningfully more coffee and losing money doing it.

Now, what's inside COGS? For a specialty chain, the standard build is green coffee beans at roughly 25% of COGS, dairy around 20%, cups and lids and packaging around 15%, food and pastry around 30%, syrups and sundries the rest. Green coffee is the one that's moved — arabica futures have been on a tear, and a chain buying spot rather than hedged would eat all of it.

So let's size it. Green coffee at 25% of COGS is 25 points of revenue, and dairy at 20% is another 20 points. Those two lines alone are 45 points of margin — which is more than enough to explain the entire 16-point swing several times over. That tells me the beans-and-dairy block is where the whole problem lives, and the remaining packaging and food lines are essentially immaterial to the diagnosis.

