[candidate · 17:09] Sure, let me broaden beyond price and procurement. A few more levers.

First, product mix engineering. Ticket is already up because people buy more items, so lean into that — push the highest-margin items: brewed coffee and espresso drinks carry far better margins than dairy-heavy or food items, which are exactly the inflated inputs. Re-merchandise the menu toward low-input-cost, high-margin SKUs, and the blended COGS ratio falls without touching headline prices.

Second, input substitution and recipe reformulation. If dairy's the pain, promote oat or other alternatives where the cost curve is more favorable, right-size portions, cut waste. Standardizing shot counts and reducing spoilage can claw back a point or two.

Third, overhead — it crept from 12 to 14. Small, but if that's corporate bloat or redundant tech spend it's pure margin, no demand risk.

Fourth, revenue-side without list-price hikes: loyalty programs, bundling, and upsell prompts to lift attach rate and ticket further, and renegotiating delivery-aggregator commissions if they're on those platforms, since that's margin leakage I flagged early.

Fifth, operational throughput — more transactions per store per hour spreads the flat labor and rent base, improving net margin even at constant unit economics.

So the full menu is: price, procurement, mix, reformulation, overhead, revenue programs, and throughput. If I had to rank, I'd lead with procurement and mix because they attack the root cause without demand risk, then selective pricing. Do we know whether they're on delivery platforms at all?
