[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 17:12] That information isn't something I have. You've laid out a full set of levers. What is your recommendation to the CEO?

[candidate · 18:42] Here's my recommendation. Brew & Bean's margin fell from 24% to 6% almost entirely because of input-cost inflation — coffee beans up 40%, dairy, packaging and food up about 37.5% — while menu prices stayed frozen for two years. COGS rose from 42% to 58% of revenue; labor and overhead are essentially not the story. So the fix must target input cost and pricing, not labor cuts.

I'd act on three fronts. First, procurement — move from spot buying to hedged contracts on beans and dairy and renegotiate volume discounts, since purchasing is up 15%. This attacks the root cause with no customer risk. Second, a measured price increase, in the range of 10%, piloted in a subset of stores first given that traffic is only up around 5% and elasticity is unknown. Sized roughly, 10% adds about $48M of revenue at minimal incremental cost, recovering on the order of 9 points of margin. Third, menu mix engineering — steer customers toward high-margin, low-input drinks to lower the blended COGS ratio without headline price hikes.

Combined, procurement plus pricing credibly moves us from 6% back toward the mid-to-high teens, with mix providing additional upside.

Key risks: price-driven volume loss given soft traffic, and continued commodity escalation if hedging isn't feasible.

Next steps: pull historical hedging posture and a component-level COGS split, run a pricing elasticity pilot in 15–20 stores, and model the blended margin under combined scenarios. I'd want two weeks on the pilot before scaling nationally.
